# Frontend Development Stages

Work in sprint order. Each sprint ends with something testable. Prefer one focused commit (or a small set) per sprint goal. Do not start a later sprint until the current one is usable.

| Stage | Sprint | Frontend commit goals |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | Setup | Next.js App Router scaffold, TypeScript, env for `API_URL` / revalidate secret, base layout, `lib/api` client stub, CI or lint scripts |
| 1 | Auth shell | `(auth)` login/register pages, httpOnly cookie session helpers, middleware stubs, guards for roles |
| 2 | Seller store UI | Store register/edit forms, manual map pin fallback UI when geocoding fails |
| 3 | Attach flows | `(seller)/attach` search, match candidates, confirmation, and new-product wizard screens wired to API |
| 4 | Public catalogue | Home, product `[slug]` (static + revalidate), sellers list (SSR by location), search with keyword-fallback messaging, store profile |
| 5 | Peer review UI | Proposal create/status, review/vote screens, version history where permitted |
| 6 | Community | Read community anonymously, verification flow UI, sentiment summary display |
| 7 | Buyer extras | Wishlist, alerts, seller analytics dashboard |
| 8 | Harden | Bug fixes, accessibility pass, empty/error states, docs for running the app |

## Stage rules

- Stage 0 is the **initial commit** baseline. Create it before feature work.
- Stages 1 to 3 can use placeholder API responses only if the matching backend stage is not ready; mark TODOs clearly.
- Stage 4 must hit real public API endpoints once backend stage 4 exists.
- Keep route folders aligned with access levels. Prefer `components/{ui,product,seller,proposal,community}` and `lib/{api,auth,location,query}`.
